 {
  "source": "Bloomberg",
  "title": "Gundlach's Bond Coupon Cut Is No Match For Inflation",
  "type": "macro",
  "priority": "medium",
  "published": "2026-05-16",
  "insights": [
   "Jeffrey Gundlach's theory that the US Treasury might restructure debt by cutting coupons is dismissed as an extreme and unlikely scenario.",
   "Inflation is currently acting as a 'liquidation' mechanism for the real value of US debt, with real interest rates on outstanding Treasury debt having recently turned negative.",
   "Financial repression is identified as the more likely government lever to manage the spiraling interest bill, acting as an implicit tax on bondholders rather than an explicit default.",
   "US interest expense is currently 20% of tax revenues and is projected to reach 30% within a decade, creating a long-term fiscal sustainability risk.",
   "The Fed's ability to control long-term yields is limited; if the market demands higher yields, the government will likely resort to regulatory measures to force borrowing at below-market rates."
